\begin{align*}
\frac{\cos x - \sin x}{\cos x + \sin x} &= \frac{1 - \tan x}{1 + \tan x}
\end{align*}
Now plugging in $\tan 4x$ in the above equation yields
\begin{align*}
\frac{1 - \tan 4x}{1 + \tan 4x} &= \frac{\cos x - \sin x}{\cos x + \sin x} \\
\tan 4x &= \frac{1 - \frac{\cos x - \sin x}{\cos x + \sin x}}{1 + \frac{\cos x - \sin x}{\cos x + \sin x}} \\
\tan 4x &= \frac{\cos x + \sin x - \cos x + \sin x}{\cos x + \sin x + \cos x - \sin x} \\
\tan 4x &= \frac{2 \sin x}{2 \cos x} \\
\tan 4x &= \tan x \\
4x &= x + k \pi, k \in \mathbb{Z} \\
x &= \frac{k \pi}{3}, k \in \mathbb{Z}
\end{align*}
So $x = \frac{\pi}{3}$ is a solution. Now let's see if there is another solution where $k$ is not a multiple of $3$. If $x = \frac{k \pi}{3}$, then $\tan x = \tan \frac{k \pi}{3}$. So to check if another solution exists, we can check if there are any values of $x$ such that $\tan 4x = \tan \frac{k \pi}{3}$ for any integer $k$. The only values of $k$ such that $4 \frac{k \pi}{3} = \frac{k \pi}{3} + \pi$ are the values when $k$ is a multiple of $3$. So the smallest positive angle is \boxed{$\frac{\pi}{3}$}.

