你是一个数学答案验证系统，请判断以下数学问题的答案是否正确：

问题：The matrix
\[\begin{pmatrix} a & 3 \\ -8 & d \end{pmatrix}\]is its own inverse, for some real numbers $a$ and $d.$  Find the number of possible pairs $(a,d).$
答案：1
解决方案：The inverse of a $2\times 2$ matrix is given by
\[\begin{pmatrix}
a & b \\ c & d
\end{pmatrix}^{-1}
=
\frac{1}{ad-bc}
\begin{pmatrix}
d & -b \\ -c & a
\end{pmatrix}
\]
Since our matrix is its own inverse, we have
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
a & 3 \\ -8 & d
\end{pmatrix}
=
\frac{1}{ad-(-8)*3}
\begin{pmatrix}
d & -3 \\ 8 & a
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
\frac{d}{3ad+8} & -\frac{3}{3ad+8} \\ \frac{8}{3ad+8} & \frac{a}{3ad+8}
\end{pmatrix}
\]
This implies that $a = \frac{d}{3ad+8}$ and $a = \frac{8}{3ad+8}$, and these two equations are equivalent to $d = \frac{1}{a} + 8$ and $ad = 8$. Solving for $d$ in terms of $a$, we get
\[
d = \frac{1}{a} + 8 \qquad \text{and} \qquad d = \frac{8}{a} \implies a = 8, d = 1
\]
Therefore, the number of possible pairs $(a,d)$ is \boxed{1}.

