问题：A math class has fewer than $40$ students. When the students try to sit in rows of $7$, $3$ students sit in the last row. When the students try to sit in rows of $5$, $1$ student is left in the last row. How many students are in this class?
答案：9
解决方案：We know that when students try to sit in rows of 7, 3 students are left over in the last row. This means that $n$ when divided by 7 leaves a remainder of 3, where $n$ is the number of students in the class. We can write this as $n \equiv 3 \pmod{7}$.

When students try to sit in rows of 5, 1 student is left over in the last row. This means that $n$ when divided by 5 leaves a remainder of 1, so $n \equiv 1 \pmod{5}$.

To solve this we use the Chinese Remainder Theorem. We need to find a number $x$ such that $x \equiv 3 \pmod{7}$ and $x \equiv 1 \pmod{5}$.

We can use the formula $x \equiv a \cdot b^{-1} \pmod{b}$ to find the inverse of $b \pmod{a}$, where $b^{-1} \cdot b \equiv 1 \pmod{a}$.

So we have $7 \cdot b^{-1} \equiv 1 \pmod{5}$. We can use the Euclidean algorithm to find that $b^{-1} \equiv 3 \pmod{5}$.

Now we can plug in $b^{-1}$ to find $x$:

\begin{align*}
x &\equiv 3 \cdot 3 \pmod{5}\\
x &\equiv 9 \pmod{5}
\end{align*}

We want to find a number that is congruent to 9 mod 5 and also congruent to 3 mod 7. One such number is $x = 9$. Let's check this:

\begin{align*}
9 &\equiv 9 \pmod{5}\\
9 &\equiv 3 \pmod{7}
\end{align*}

Therefore, $n = 9$ satisfies the given conditions. So there are $\boxed{9}$ students in this math class.

