If the graph of $y=h(x-3)$ is drawn on the same set of axes as the graph above, then the two graphs intersect at one point. What is the sum of the coordinates of that point?
答案：(0,0)
解决方案：To understand $h(x-3)$, we need to think of the $x-3$ coordinate and then slide it by a factor of 3.
So, if we take 0 as the starting point, then the input of $0-3$ is $x=-3$. 
From the graph, we can find the corresponding $y$ to this $x=-3$. This is $-2$. 
Hence, the point (?3,?2) on the graph. Similarly, for $x=3$, $y=2$.
From the graph, we can also figure out that $h(x)$ is symmetrical around $y=x$. 
Hence, to get the intersecting point, we need to take the midpoint of the two endpoints.
$(0,-2)+(0,2)=(0,0)$. So, the intersecting point is at \boxed{(0,0)} on the graph.
